    "description": "The tax-reporting platform is replacing the untested ReportingEngine that reads monthly CSV exports from the TreasuryGateway and emits formatted PDF summaries. Production behavior is known mainly from support investigations: blank account codes sometimes remain blank, currency symbols vary by locale, and rows with malformed dates are skipped. The nightly ReportingJob must continue producing byte-identical PDFs while the engine is split into parser, calculation, and rendering components. There is no authoritative specification, and the team cannot interview the original author. Before refactoring, engineers need to replay a representative corpus of stored TreasuryGateway exports through the untouched engine and commit every observable result, including report text, row ordering, skipped records, and logged warnings. The new components must be checked against those committed reference artifacts on every build, even when a deliberate business change later makes the old behavior undesirable.",
    "decision_statement": "Decide whether to preserve the ReportingEngine's undocumented production behavior with committed reference artifacts before changing its internals, rather than write assertions from a guessed specification.",
    "notes": "Replaying stored exports through the untouched engine and committing the observables as the build baseline (characterization-test) is the decision; consumer-driven-contract-test is the near-miss as the family's only sibling."
  },
